alternatives in the trial and it must  also be voluntary or free from coercion  or other undue influences so in the case  of therapeutic research if we look at  the chart on the left of the slide from  we we can ask a question right so we can  say why would patients agree to incur  additional risk by enrolling in the  trial and there's a fairly obvious or  reasonable answer that  that makes informed consent possible  right so it could be very reasonable for  someone to take on additional risk to  their health for the possibility of  therapeutic benefit even if that  possibility is small right and in many  cases enrolling in these types of trials  is in fact in the best clinical interest  of those participants but if we focus on  the non therapeutic case or the chart on  the right we don't have available that  same kind of answer because of course  there currently is no direct therapeutic  benefit for those participants so we  might feel like we're in a bit of a  puzzle right so we need to get informed  free consent so we're looking for a  reason for enrolling in these trials  that could be informed and free but we  can't use the same reason we use for the  therapeutic cases so we might start to  wonder whether there is a reason that  could be reasonably informed and free  and it has been suggested that the  presence of altruistic motives could  provide such a reason  so we know that patients may participate  or agree to enroll in trials for all  kinds of reasons right so it might be  that participants actually are agreeing  to enroll in non therapeutic trials  because they mistakenly believe that the  trial is therapeutic that could be a  reason or they could enroll because they  have feelings of debt to their surgeon  they feel like they can't say no but  notice that those reasons are ethically  concerning right it would raise some red  flags about the possibility of informed  and free consent but altruism on the  other hand and the presence of an  altruistic motive at least at first  glance doesn't have those same kinds of  problems right so the participant says  look I know I won't benefit and I know  my condition will not improve but I want  to help Society and I care very much  about helping scientists understand the  brain that kind of reason right actually  looks like it's based on a proper  understanding with the goals of the  trial and doesn't yet on its own give us  cause for concern about coercion and  undue influence so the next way in which  altruism may affect the ethics of these  types of trials is in talking about the  risk benefit assessments of these trials  so one of our other pillars of research  ethics is that the risks be reasonable  in relation to the benefits right so the  federal common rule requires IRB s to  determine that risks are reasonable in  relation to anticipated benefits if any  two subjects and the importance of the  knowledge that may reasonably be  expected to result so IRB IRB committees  on ask or an explicit delineation right  of the rest of the trial benefit to the  individual benefits to society and a  risk benefit analysis but in the case of  non therapeutic invasive neurologic  research the mouthful right applying  this rule is particularly difficult  right for a few reasons  so the first is that the relative rarity  of the investigative techniques that are  used in these types of trials can result  in a kind of uncertainty about what the  actual risks are and that fact  compounded with the fat  that harm to the brain right or  neurological arm may be more  consequential than harm to other organs  can give us pause for concern right so  we have some challenges here and really  being able to delineate the rest of a  trial in a meaningful way on the benefit  side of things you know we might ask you  know how how are we supposed to  appropriately value say a piece of  scientific knowledge how much weight do  we give that benefit right or an  expected increase in knowledge about  some particular part of the brain right  how do we value that and there's also  questions about what ought to count as a  benefit right so currently as the  federal common rule states we're only  really considering direct therapeutic  benefits to the participant or else  benefits to society but it is kind of an  open question whether there are other  types of benefits we ought to consider  and I'll return to that shortly  the last challenge which I'll just  mention briefly is that we have to then  work out what a reasonable ratio would  be and in the case of non therapeutic  studies we're trying to balance personal  risk with benefit to society and it's  not at all clear what would actually  count as a reasonable ratio what how  much risk personal risk is actually  reasonable in relation to how much  knowledge about the brain so I'll add  that currently you know when there is no  direct benefit to the individual right  the only other benefit that we consider  in conducting this assessment is benefit  to society and given that there's quite  a lot of non therapeutic research  happening that implicitly suggests that  in many cases the benefits to society  are sufficient to outweigh the risk but  it's worth pausing to ask yourself you  know whether that's justified or on what  grounds is that actually true so to kind  of tie that into our case about altruism  it has also been argued that altruism  may count as a kind of benefit to the  participants in the trial so it may be  that being able to pursue your goals  goals that you care about or your  altruistic goals may contribute to an  increase in your well-being  overall well-being right and it may very  well be that an increase in your  well-being ought to count as a kind of  benefit in these types of assessments so  just as you know being able to donate  money to a cause that you care deeply  about might possibly increase your  quality of life or your sense of  well-being being able to participate in  the trial whose goal you care deeply  about may also contribute to your  well-being so you know it's it's an open  question I won't argue whether in fact  we should count altruism as a benefit  but it's an open question and we're  already using these concepts in our  ethical assessments and it's worth  pausing to consider whether altruism  ought to count it's also worth noting  that in clinical medicine right we care  you know we care about more than simply  survival right we also care about  quality of life and it's plausible that  an increase in well-being is intimately  connected to an increase in your quality  of life it's worth pausing also to  reflect on you know what we have to face  if we don't count altruism as a kind of  benefit on that risk risk benefit scale  right without altruism or without any  direct benefit in our best case  scenarios the risk benefit assessment in  non therapeutic research is I would say  ethically concerning right how is it  permissible to allow someone to take all  of the risk for zero benefit is really  the situation we're facing and the  standard answer to this is to appeal  back to consent  look we allow people to consent to  minimal risk with no benefit because we  value autonomy right there's one of the  explanation that's given but notice that  this explanation doesn't say that the  risk benefit ratio in these cases is  reasonable in fact it just explains the  ratio or or justifies the ratio by  appealing back to consent so notice that  if altruism weren't account as a kind of  benefit it would be a way to kind of add  to the benefit and change that risk  benefit ratio directly so that might be  a kind of ethical positive for us so so  far it might start to seem like I'm  building a case for altruism in fact  being  a golden motive right maybe it helps  with informed consent maybe it helps  promote beneficence towards the  participants themselves but I would just  you know for the last part of the talk  pause to remind ourselves that the  presence of altruistic motives while  maybe it's promising in these other  areas does not absolve us of all of our  robust ethical concerns and one concern  that I'll talk about briefly is  exploitation right so this is another  color of research ethics to protect  research participants from exploitation  and we can see its surface here in this  quote from a manual when learning  grading I'll just read the first part of  it the overarching objective of clinical  research is to develop generalizable  understanding of human biology subjects  who participate are the means to  securing stuck knowledge by placing some  people at risk of harm for the good of  others  clinical research has the potential for  exploitation of human subjects so  exploitation is commonly taken to be  taking unfair advantage of a person's  vulnerability for the sake of advancing  one's own end and the point here is  pretty simple right so the presence of  altruistic motives just does not on its  own preclude the possibility of being  exploited so we still have a lot of work  to do regardless of how the first part  of this talk turns out and the point is  simply that norms about exploitation do  not reduce to norms about informed  consent or norms about arrests a  favorable risk benefit ratio especially  in the non therapeutic case that's just  the kind of a complicated way of saying  that you can be exploited or treated  unfairly in enrolling in the trial even  when you consent to that treatment right  and you can be exploited or treated  unfairly in enrolling in a trial even if  the risks to you are reasonable in  relation to the benefits to society um I  would add that if altruism does count as  a benefit we should take into account  for these trials then maybe that is  helping a little bit with exploitation  because then in these cases the  participant is getting something a  direct benefit in some way but it still  doesn't absolve us of all of our worries  for the following reason  and the first of this so it's plausible  that there are ethical limits to  altruistic sacrifice right and that not  all such sacrifice is reasonable and so  then if this is so taking advantage of  someone's willingness to make those  sacrifices maybe wrong it's also  possible that altruistic behavior may  stem from feelings of self-loathing and  self-worth and so taking advantage of  someone's willingness to make those  sacrifices may also be wrong now this  can kind of compound with the fact that  participant populations in these cases  are already highly vulnerable suffering  suffering from debilitating neurological  disorder disorder and many are also  socially isolated so now that we've  walked through some of the ethical  issues I'd like to end with some  practical concerns the first are some  epistemic worries right that we don't  have well-developed tools for assessing  altruistic motivation so even when  patients report altruistic motivations  we don't currently know how to assess  those in light of some of the other  worries about vulnerability and  exploitation and we also know that there  are many factors that may influence  patients reporting altruistic motives  such as the belief that they will be  more likely to be accepted into the  trial if they report being altruistic or  the belief that they ought to be  altruistic so we have some worries here  about being able to really tell when it  is that patients are altruistic and  given the ethics it really seems to  matter that we get it right so now I'll  turn it over to Nader to talk about some  practical issues so as we design studies  in the future we have to both be aware  of these concerns about altruism but  also learn more about it so future  studies should really impact our  understanding of how participation  changes the subject sense of well-being  as well as their sense of valued Society  we need to really reflect as well on our  consent process and how it enables or  might complicate our understanding of  patients altruistic motives thank you  very much for this opportunity thank you  to pretend and dr. fine singer for that  compelling presentation please remember  to submit your questions for dr. Ponton  and dr. fine singer will answer them in  the live battle Q&amp;A taking place today  at 11:20 a.m. Pacific time 2:20 p.m.  Eastern Time  please join us for this unique  opportunity to interact directly with  our renowned experts to discuss in our  ethics and the ethical considerations of  using new newer technologies we hope to  see you there coming up next dr. Francis  Shen will continue the neuroethics track  and present from lab to law neuroscience  in the heart worm
 

